# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google sign-in (Auth.js v5)
#
# Leave these unset and RE:VEAL runs in demo mode: everything works against the
# seeded profile and the sign-in screen explains what is missing.
#
# To turn on real accounts:
#
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#      Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
#
#   2. Authorised redirect URIs  -- required. The path is fixed by Auth.js and
#      Google matches the whole URI exactly.
#        http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#        https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/google
#
#   3. Authorised JavaScript origins -- NOT required: the token exchange runs
#      on the server, not in the browser. Only needed if you add One Tap.
#        http://localhost:3000
#        https://<your-domain>
#
#   4. Copy the client ID and secret below.
#
#   5. Generate a session-signing secret:
#        npx auth secret
#      or
#        openssl rand -base64 32
#
#   6. Verify before starting the app:
#        npm run check:auth -- https://<your-domain>
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
AUTH_SECRET=

# The app trusts the request host so it runs locally and on any host. If you
# deploy behind a proxy that does not normalise the Host header, pin the URL
# here instead — it takes precedence.
# AUTH_URL=https://your-domain.example

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase — where conversations are stored
#
# Leave these unset and messaging falls back to the seeded, read-only inbox:
# the composer still works but says plainly that nothing is being saved.
#
# Set them and messages persist, survive a reload, and reach the other
# participants.
#
#   1. https://supabase.com/dashboard → your project → Settings → API Keys
#   2. Project URL            → SUPABASE_URL
#   3. service_role secret    → SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#
# The service role key bypasses row-level security, so it is server-only:
# src/lib/supabase/server.ts is marked `server-only`, and the messaging tables
# have RLS on with no policies, which denies the publishable key outright.
# Never expose this key to the browser and never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
